('In this course, citrate synthase (acetyl CoA + oxaloacetate → citrate, the first step of the citric acid cycle) is classified as which enzyme class?',['ligase','hydrolase','isomerase','oxidoreductase'],'Two molecules (acetyl CoA and oxaloacetate) are joined into one larger molecule, citrate.','Citrate synthase joins two molecules, acetyl CoA (2 C) and oxaloacetate (4 C), into one larger molecule, citrate (6 C), so this course classifies it as a <b>ligase</b>.'),
('How many molecules of CO<sub>2</sub> are released in one turn of the citric acid cycle?',['2','1','3','6'],'Two carbons come in as acetyl CoA each turn.','Two carbons enter as acetyl CoA and <b>two CO<sub>2</sub></b> leave (at the isocitrate and α-ketoglutarate steps).'),
('Where in the mitochondrion are the complexes of the electron transport chain located?',['the inner mitochondrial membrane','the outer membrane','the matrix, dissolved','the cytosol'],'The chain must build a proton gradient across a membrane.','The complexes are embedded in the <b>inner mitochondrial membrane</b> (the cristae).'),
('During electron transport, H<sup>+</sup> ions are pumped',['from the matrix into the intermembrane space, then flow back through ATP synthase','from the cytosol into the nucleus','out of the cell','into the matrix, then out through complex I'],'ATP synthase lets the protons return. Where do they return to?','The complexes pump H<sup>+</sup> <b>into the intermembrane space</b>; the protons flow back into the matrix through ATP synthase, which makes ATP (chemiosmosis).'),
