('Oxidative phosphorylation means that ATP is made',['by ATP synthase, using energy from electron transport','by transferring phosphate from a substrate to ADP','by breaking down glycogen','by hydrolyzing ATP'],'Which process links oxidation (electron transport) to phosphorylation?','Energy released as electrons pass along the chain builds a proton gradient that drives <b>ATP synthase</b>: oxidative phosphorylation.'),
('The citric acid cycle does not use O<sub>2</sub> directly, yet it stops when O<sub>2</sub> runs out. Why?',['NAD<sup>+</sup> and FAD are no longer regenerated by electron transport','O<sub>2</sub> is a substrate of citrate synthase','CO<sub>2</sub> builds up in the matrix','ATP synthase starts running backward'],'Where does the cycle get its oxidized coenzymes?','The cycle needs NAD<sup>+</sup> and FAD; without O<sub>2</sub> the electron transport chain cannot reoxidize NADH and FADH<sub>2</sub>, so the cycle stops.'),
('Complete oxidation of glucose gives about 32 ATP in liver cells but about 30 ATP in muscle and brain cells. Why?',['the two cytosolic NADH from glycolysis enter the electron transport chain by different shuttles','muscle cells make less pyruvate','brain cells lack the citric acid cycle','liver mitochondria have more ATP synthase per NADH'],'Cytosolic NADH cannot cross the inner membrane itself.','In liver and heart the malate–aspartate shuttle delivers the electrons as NADH (2.5 ATP each); in muscle and brain the glycerol-phosphate shuttle delivers them as FADH<sub>2</sub> (1.5 ATP each).'),
('NAD<sup>+</sup>, which carries electrons to the electron transport chain, is made from which vitamin?',['niacin (vitamin B<sub>3</sub>)','riboflavin (vitamin B<sub>2</sub>)','vitamin C','vitamin K'],'NAD stands for nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide.','Nicotinamide comes from <b>niacin</b>; FAD comes from riboflavin.'),
('Fat provides about 9 kcal g⁻¹ but carbohydrate only about 4 kcal g⁻¹. Why?',['the carbons of fat are more reduced (more C–H, fewer C–O bonds), so their oxidation yields more NADH and FADH<sub>2</sub>','fat contains nitrogen','carbohydrates are not oxidized by the citric acid cycle','fat molecules are smaller'],'Which is already partly oxidized: a carbon with an –OH, or a CH<sub>2</sub>?','Carbohydrate carbons already carry oxygen; fat carbons are mostly CH<sub>2</sub>, so each has more electrons to give to NAD<sup>+</sup> and FAD.'),
('As electrons move along the electron transport chain, most of the energy they release is first stored as',['a proton (H<sup>+</sup>) gradient across the inner membrane','glycogen','heat only','NADH'],'ATP synthase is powered by something the chain builds.','The energy is captured as a <b>proton gradient</b>; ATP synthase then converts part of it into ATP and the rest is released as heat.'),
('Why are the brain and heart damaged within minutes when their oxygen supply stops?',['they depend on oxidative phosphorylation for most of their ATP and have little stored energy','they make ATP only by glycolysis','oxygen is a structural part of their proteins','they cannot carry out the citric acid cycle'],'Compare the ATP yield of glycolysis alone with complete oxidation.','Without O<sub>2</sub>, electron transport and <b>oxidative phosphorylation</b> stop and ATP output falls to the small yield of glycolysis.'),
('Put the stages of complete glucose oxidation in order.',['glycolysis → pyruvate oxidation → citric acid cycle → electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation','citric acid cycle → glycolysis → electron transport → pyruvate oxidation','pyruvate oxidation → glycolysis → electron transport → citric acid cycle','electron transport → citric acid cycle → glycolysis → pyruvate oxidation'],'Glucose is split first, in the cytosol.','Glucose → pyruvate (glycolysis) → acetyl CoA (pyruvate oxidation) → citric acid cycle → NADH and FADH<sub>2</sub> used by <b>electron transport</b> and oxidative phosphorylation.'),
